<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick II, Elector of Brandenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1413">1413</a>–<a href="/years/1471">1471</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Frederick Irontooth · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick II, called Irontooth, was Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> from 1440 to 1470, son of Frederick I. He broke the independence of the twin towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colln">Cölln</a> in 1448 and began a castle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> that became the Hohenzollern residence. He also bought back the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumark">Neumark</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in 1455, and abdicated in favour of his brother Albert <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-elector-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="Frederick II, Elector of Brandenburg" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arolsen_Klebeband_01_203_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom inherited from the Normans and <b>Frederick II</b> a centralised royal administration, with justiciars over the provinces, a royal treasury and a high court, but the Angevins granted extensive lands and jurisdiction to French and local barons, and royal authority depended heavily on these lords.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Frederick II</b>, known as stupor mundi, wrote a treatise on falconry and patronised the Sicilian School of poets, whose love lyrics in a Sicilian vernacular produced the sonnet, usually credited to Giacomo da Lentini, and influenced Dante and Petrarch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…of Nuremberg, of the House of Hohenzollern, who became Elector Frederick I. His successors subdued the towns, with <b>Frederick II Irontooth</b> breaking Berlin’s independence in 1448, and Albert Achilles laid down in the Dispositio Achillea of 1473 that the Mark itself should pass undivided to the eldest son.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The commune remained formally part of the Holy Roman Empire, but emperors such as Frederick Barbarossa and <b>Frederick II</b> found it hard to control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tangermünde, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Neustadt an der Aisch, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.54,11.97&amp;year=1450" aria-label="Tangermünde on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4818 351 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick II, Elector of Brandenburg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5268 621h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5268 621h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.54,11.97&amp;year=1450">What was here in 1450? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
